1. JWT Authentication Bypass via jwt.decode()
Concept

JWT ke saath do operations hoti hain:

Decode
jwt.decode(token)

Sirf token ko read karta hai.

Signature verify nahi karta.
Koi bhi token accept kar lega.

Example:

{
  "sub": "administrator"
}

Agar attacker payload modify kare:

{
  "sub": "administrator"
}

aur signature invalid ho tab bhi decode() data read kar lega.

Verify
jwt.verify(token, secret)

Ye:

Signature verify karta hai.
Secret/Public Key check karta hai.
Expiration check karta hai.

Tabhi trusted data return karta hai.

Vulnerable Code
const user = jwt.decode(token);

if(user.sub === "administrator")
{
    grantAccess();
}

Developer sirf decode kar raha hai.

Signature verify nahi kar raha.

Attack Flow
Attacker
    |
    v
JWT Payload Change
sub = administrator
    |
    v
Invalid Signature
    |
    v
Server uses decode()
    |
    v
Admin Access
Impact
Authentication Bypass
Privilege Escalation
Account Takeover
Detection

Look for:

jwt.decode()

instead of

jwt.verify()
2. JWT Accepting "alg":"none"
Concept

JWT header contains algorithm.

Normal:

{
  "alg": "HS256"
}

Some vulnerable servers trust:

{
  "alg": "none"
}

Meaning:

No Signature Required
Attack

Original:

{
  "sub": "wiener"
}

Change:

{
  "sub": "administrator"
}

Header:

{
  "alg": "none"
}

Remove signature.

Result

Server says:

alg = none
No need to verify signature

Admin access granted.

Lab Steps
Intercept JWT.
Change:
{
 "alg":"none"
}
Payload:
{
 "sub":"administrator"
}
Remove signature.
Send.
Visit:
/admin
Delete Carlos.

Lab solved.

Root Cause

Server trusts user supplied algorithm.

Should never trust:

{
 "alg":"none"
}
3. JWT Weak Secret Brute Force (Hashcat)
Concept

HS256 uses symmetric key.

Server Secret
=
Signing Secret
=
Verification Secret

Example:

secret123

If secret weak hai:

password
admin
secret
123456

Bruteforce possible.

Attack Flow
Get JWT
   |
   v
Extract Signature
   |
   v
Hashcat
   |
   v
Find Secret
   |
   v
Create New JWT
   |
   v
Admin Access
Hashcat

Mode:

hashcat -m 16500 jwt.txt wordlist.txt

Common wordlists:

rockyou.txt
jwt.secrets.list
After Secret Found

Example:

secret = football

Create new token:

{
  "sub":"administrator"
}

Sign with:

football

Server accepts.

Root Cause

Weak secret key.

Bad:

password
secret
admin123

Good:

128-bit random key
256-bit random key
4. JWK Header Injection
Concept

Normally server stores public keys.

JWT header may contain:

{
  "jwk":{
      ...
  }
}

Some servers directly trust this key.

Attack

Generate own RSA key pair.

Private Key:

Used by attacker

Public Key:

Inserted into JWT

Header:

{
  "alg":"RS256",
  "jwk":{
      "kty":"RSA",
      ...
  }
}

Payload:

{
  "sub":"administrator"
}

Sign using private key.

Result

Server:

Reads attacker's public key
Verifies attacker's signature
Accepts token

Admin access.

Detection

Look for:

{
  "jwk":{ ... }
}

inside JWT header.

Root Cause

Server trusts user supplied verification key.

5. JKU Header Injection
Concept

JKU = JSON Web Key Set URL

Header:

{
  "alg":"RS256",
  "jku":"https://keys.company.com/jwks.json"
}

Server:

Downloads public keys
Uses them for verification
Vulnerability

Developer allows attacker-controlled URL.

Attack

Create own JWKS:

{
  "keys":[
      {
         "kty":"RSA",
         "kid":"attack"
      }
  ]
}

Host it on exploit server.

Example:

https://exploit-server.net/jwks.json

Header:

{
  "alg":"RS256",
  "jku":"https://exploit-server.net/jwks.json",
  "kid":"attack"
}

Payload:

{
  "sub":"administrator"
}

Sign using corresponding private key.

Result

Server:

Downloads attacker's public key
Verifies attacker's signature
Accepts token

Admin access.

Attack Flow
Attacker Private Key
        |
        v
JWT Sign
        |
        v
JKU -> Exploit Server
        |
        v
Server Downloads Public Key
        |
        v
Signature Valid
        |
        v
Admin Access
Root Cause

No whitelist on JKU URLs.

Should only allow:

company-owned domains
6. KID Injection Attack
Concept

KID = Key ID

Example:

{
  "kid":"key1"
}

Server:

readKey(kid)

or

SELECT key FROM keys WHERE kid='key1'
Attack Type 1: SQL Injection
{
  "kid":"' OR 1=1--"
}

If backend vulnerable:

SELECT key FROM keys
WHERE kid='' OR 1=1--'

Returns arbitrary key.

Attack Type 2: Directory Traversal

Header:

{
  "kid":"../../../../dev/null"
}

Server:

readFile(kid)

Reads:

/dev/null
Why /dev/null?

Contents:

(empty)

Server uses:

secret = ""

Attacker signs JWT using:

empty string

Signature becomes valid.

Attack Flow
JWT
 |
 v
kid=../../../../dev/null
 |
 v
Server Reads Empty File
 |
 v
Secret=""
 |
 v
Attacker Signs With ""
 |
 v
Valid Signature
 |
 v
Admin Access